    pro_tips_95: 'Official JAMB & WAEC Syllabus Concept: Focus on core definitions, formulas, and past questions.',
    syllabus_objectives: 'Candidates should be able to master the fundamental principles of Arms Of Government in line with the official syllabus.',
    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<h2><strong>Lesson Objective</strong></h2><p>By the end of the lesson, students should be able to:</p><ul><li>Identify the three arms of government.</li><li>Explain the functions of each arm.</li><li>Relate each arm of government to its role in Nigeria.</li></ul><p>Nigeria's government is divided into <strong> three main arms</strong>:</p><ol><li><strong>The Executive</strong></li><li><strong>The Legislature</strong></li><li><strong>The Judiciary</strong></li></ol><h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Executive Arm</strong></h1><p>The <strong> Executive</strong> is responsible for implementing government policies and managing the day-to-day affairs of the country.</p><p><strong>Key Officials:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>President</strong> – Head of state and head of government at the federal level.</li><li><strong>Vice President</strong></li><li><strong>State Governors</strong> – Head the executive at the state level.</li><li><strong>Deputy Governors</strong></li><li><strong>Ministers and Advisers</strong></li><li><strong>Secretary to the State Government</strong></li><li><strong>Civil Service</strong></li><li><strong>Police and Armed Forces</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Functions of the Executive:</strong></p><ul><li>Implementing laws passed by the legislature.</li><li>Appointing top government officials.</li><li>Giving assent to bills.</li><li>Preparing the national budget.</li><li>Maintaining law and order.</li><li>Providing social amenities (e.g., roads, schools, electricity).</li><li>Recommending policies.</li><li>Declaring war (when necessary).</li><li>Defending the country from external threats.</li></ul><p><strong>Types of Executive:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Single/Presidential Executive:</strong> All executive powers are vested in the President (e.g., Nigeria, USA).</li><li><strong>Dual/Parliamentary Executive:</strong> One person is Head of State, and another is Head of Government (e.g., United Kingdom).</li><li><strong>Collegiate Executive:</strong> A group shares executive power, with rotating leadership (e.g., used in Togo during Olympio's rule).</li></ul><h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Judiciary Arm</strong></h1><p>The <strong> Judiciary</strong> interprets laws and ensures justice is served. It operates independently to apply the law to individual cases.</p><p><strong>Structure of the Judiciary in Nigeria:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Supreme Court</strong> – The highest court in the country</li><li><strong>Court of Appeal</strong></li><li><strong>Federal High Court</strong></li><li><strong>State High Courts</strong></li><li><strong>Magistrate Courts</strong></li><li><strong>Customary and Sharia Courts</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Functions of the Judiciary:</strong></p><ul><li>Interpretation of laws.</li><li>Judicial review of government actions.</li><li>Settlement of disputes.</li><li>Punishment of offenders.</li><li>Protection of individual rights.</li><li>Contribution to law-making through legal judgments.</li><li>Prevention of unlawful acts.</li></ul><h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Legislative Arm</strong></h1><p>The <strong> Legislature</strong> is responsible for making laws that guide both government actions and citizen behavior. It may be called the <strong> Parliament</strong>, <strong> Congress</strong>, or <strong> National Assembly</strong>, depending on the country.</p><p><strong>Structure in Nigeria:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>National Assembly</strong> (Federal Level) <ul><li><strong>Senate (Upper House)</strong> – Led by the Senate President</li><li><strong>House of Representatives (Lower House)</strong> – Led by the Speaker</li></ul></li><li><strong>State Houses of Assembly</strong> – Each state has its own legislature led by a Speaker</li></ul><p><strong>Functions of the Legislature:</strong></p><ul><li>Law-making.</li><li>Reviewing and approving government policies.</li><li>Controlling public funds and expenditures.</li><li>Power to impeach public officials.</li><li>Oversight and monitoring of government activities.</li><li>Amending the Constitution.</li></ul><h2><strong>Types of Legislature:</strong></h2><p><strong>a. Unicameral Legislature</strong></p><p>Only one legislative chamber exists.</p><p><em>Examples:</em> Ghana, Sierra Leone, Gambia, Kenya, Cameroon</p><p><strong>Advantages:</strong></p><ul><li>Faster law-making.</li><li>Cost-effective.</li><li>Suitable for smaller countries.</li></ul><p><strong>b. Bicameral Legislature</strong></p><p>Two legislative chambers – usually an upper and a lower house.</p><p><em>Examples:</em> Nigeria, Malaysia, Canada, Germany, India, Brazil</p><p><strong>Advantages:</strong></p><ul><li>Checks and balances.</li><li>Better scrutiny of laws.</li><li>Broader representation.</li><li>More experienced lawmakers.</li></ul>`
